%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"**Table of Contents**"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_CBA837BD] ## Problem 1:
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:3449)
%[text]{"align":"center"} An [axolotl](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Axolotl2.jpg)
%[text] The axolotl is an amphibian that can regrow limbs and organs. This amazing ability makes the axolotl a popular species to study in the aging and regenerative medicine field. Get the reference mtDNA sequence for the axolotl (Ambystoma mexicanum) from [GenBank](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/genbank/) using [`getgenbank(`)](https://www.mathworks.com/help/bioinfo/ref/getgenbank.html):
%[text] ***Hint:*** You'll need to go to the GenBank website and figure out the accession number first.
%[text]{"align":"center"} Research: ![Screenshot 2023-08-22 144135.png](text:image:75a8)
axolotl = getgenbank("NC_005797", "sequenceonly", true); % get the sequence from GenBank
%[text] Using this sequence:
%[text] (a) plot a pie chart or bar chart of the number of nucleotides in the sequence (remember that you can use [`basecount()`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/bioinfo/ref/basecount.html))
basecount (axolotl, "Chart", "pie") %[output:71e6d040] %[output:4cc28ff9]
%[text] (b) make the[ `ntdensity(`) ](https://www.mathworks.com/help/bioinfo/ref/ntdensity.html)plot for the sequence.
ntdensity(axolotl) %[output:02a0ca97]
%[text] ![](text:image:8f60) What is the GC content compared to the AT content for the axolotl mitochondrial DNA?
%[text] Students should note that as in the platypus example, GC content is much lower than AT content across the entire length of the sequence.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_01B1C340] ## Problem 2:
%[text] Make a phylogenetic tree for the [*hominadae*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hominidae) family. This family is also known as the 'great apes' and includes gorillas and modern humans.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:436d) ![](text:image:9717) ![](text:image:12c1)
%[text]{"align":"center"} [Gorillas](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gorilla_(13645147193).jpg), [orangutans](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Orangutan_with_infant.jpg), and [chimpanzees](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Selfie,_autorretrato_(In_Explore)_-_Flickr_-_Free_Photos,_CC0.jpg) are all members of the family hominadae
%[text] This code downloads `primates.mat` into the project's `Data/` folder if needed, then loads it into your workspace (make sure you run this section if you don't see `primates` in your workspace). This code pulls a structure called `primates` from another [MATLAB® example](https://www.mathworks.com/help/bioinfo/ug/building-a-phylogenetic-tree-for-the-hominidae-species.html). Read through this example to learn more about how to construct phylogenetic trees in MATLAB!
wd = fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,"Data");
if ~isfolder(wd)
    mkdir(wd)
end
if ~isfile(fullfile(wd,"primates.mat"))
    openExample("bioinfo/primatesdemo", "supportingFile", "primates.mat", "workDir", wd);
end
load(fullfile(wd,"primates.mat"),"primates") %[output:836830fc]
%[text] `primates` is a structure that is set up in the same way as the data we used in [Part 2](<matlab: edit ../../Scripts/S2ComparativeGenetics.m>)--with a header giving species name and a sequence for each species.
%[text] First, find the distance between species using [`seqpdist()`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/bioinfo/ref/seqpdist.html)`:`
distSeq = seqpdist(primates) %[output:691cdca6]
%[text] Make a tree object using [`seqlinkage():`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/bioinfo/ref/seqlinkage.html)
primateTree = seqlinkage(distSeq, "single", primates);
%[text] And finally plot that tree:
plot(primateTree) %[output:67b6980f]
%[text] ![](text:image:5706) What does this tree tell you about the *hominadae* family?
%[text] Students can make any reasonable observations about this tree (e.g. Neanderthals are the most closely related to humans, which broke off most recently from various species of chimps, and so on).
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_25AE91E5] ## Problem 3:
%[text] Simulate a dataset that contains SNP data across 1000 SNP positions for 3 populations (use `simulatePopulation(3)`):
[genotype, Psize, popname]  =  simulatePopulation(3);
%[text] Concatenate your population data into one matrix using `vertcat`:
allgenotype = vertcat(genotype{1}, genotype{2}, genotype{3});
%[text] Use the function [`pca()`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/stats/pca.html) to reduce the dimensions of this data:
[~, s] = pca(allgenotype);
%[text] Make a scatter plot of this reduced data (don't forget the x and y labels!):
scatter(s(:,1), s(:,2)) %[output:8f462133]
xlabel ("dimension 1") %[output:8f462133]
ylabel ("dimension 2") %[output:8f462133]
%[text] ***Bonus:*** plot the data corresponding to each population in different colors:
gscatter(s(:,1), s(:,2), popname) %[output:905fa5df]
xlabel ("dimension 1") %[output:905fa5df]
ylabel ("dimension 2") %[output:905fa5df]
%[text] ![](text:image:9f44) Think about how genetically similar or different your three populations are from one another.
%%
